<TopicPage slug="subjects/human-resources/foundational/hr-s-role-and-legal-foundations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Human resources management is the organisational function for the people system:
    acquiring, developing, rewarding and fairly treating the workforce. Its foundation is
    legal as much as managerial, because employment decisions are among the most litigated
    acts in business: every hire, firing, promotion and pay decision is a potential lawsuit,
    and the floor beneath every HR practice is the employment and anti-discrimination law of
    its jurisdiction. The modern profession therefore holds two jobs at once — <em>designing</em>
    people systems that create business value, and <em>guarding</em> the legal and ethical
    rails those systems must run on. Neither job works without the other: an HR function
    that only advises on law is clerical, and one that ignores law is a liability factory.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does the field deserve a profession of its own? Three reasons, in ascending order of
    controversy. First, labour is most firms' largest controllable cost and their least
    replicable advantage — products and patents can be copied, a workforce's capability and
    culture cannot. Second, people decisions are where an organisation's values become real:
    whatever the mission statement says, the hiring criteria, pay structures and discipline
    practices <em>are</em> the values. Third, the employment relationship is a site of
    structural power asymmetry, which societies have deliberately filled with law — and HR
    is the function that operationalises that law daily. This page covers the craft's
    domains, the legal architecture, and the professional ethics that resolve the tensions
    between them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the legal architecture under every people decision" widget="equationStep" config={lawCfg} />

  <h2>What HR does: the domains of the craft</h2>
  <p>
    The profession's knowledge body (SHRM's BASK — the BoCK's successor — organises it into
    <em>people</em>, <em>organisation</em> and <em>workplace</em>) sorts the work into
    recognisable domains. <strong>Workforce planning and talent acquisition</strong>:
    forecasting how many people with which skills the strategy needs, and running the
    sourcing, screening and selection machinery — including the science of selection
    (validated tests and structured interviews outperform unstructured ones by wide
    margins). <strong>Learning and development</strong>: onboarding, skills building,
    leadership pipelines. <strong>Total rewards</strong>: pay structures, incentives and
    benefits, designed for both market competitiveness and internal fairness.
    <strong>Performance management</strong>: goal setting, feedback, appraisal — the
    discipline's most re-invented domain. <strong>Employee and labour relations</strong>:
    discipline, grievances, unions and works councils. <strong>Health, safety and
    well-being</strong>. And <strong>HR analytics</strong> running through all of it:
    turnover, cost-per-hire, engagement, predictive attrition. A useful mental model is Dave
    Ulrich's four roles — strategic partner, administrative expert, employee champion, change
    agent — which capture why the function spans boardroom and break room.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two structural facts surprise newcomers. First, <strong>line managers own people
    decisions; HR designs the system and guards the rails</strong> — HR rarely "does" the
    hiring or firing, it makes the hiring and firing lawful, consistent and competent.
    Second, the value case is <em>evidential</em>, and a worked example shows how. Take a
    200-person firm with 20 per cent voluntary turnover: 40 departures a year. Replacement
    costs (recruiting, onboarding, lost productivity, manager time) are commonly estimated
    at half to twice an employee's annual salary; at a conservative 75 per cent on a
    $50,000 average salary, that is $37,500 per departure — $1.5 million a year, most of it
    invisible because no invoice arrives. Halving regrettable turnover (better selection,
    onboarding, management) is a seven-figure intervention, which is why modern HR argues
    with numbers and why the clerical caricature survives only in organisations that never
    measured.
  </p>

  <h2>Employment law basics: the floor under every decision</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>employment relationship</strong> itself is the first legal variable. The US
    default is <strong>employment at will</strong>: either party may end the relationship at
    any time, for any reason — except an <em>illegal</em> one (discrimination, retaliation)
    or one that violates public policy or an implied contract. Most of Europe runs on
    contract-and-statute: dismissal requires cause or redundancy plus mandated process and
    notice, which is why "process matters" is a different discipline on each side of the
    Atlantic. In both regimes, <strong>anti-discrimination law is the universal floor</strong>:
    decisions about hiring, pay, promotion and firing must be job-related, and protected
    characteristics — race, sex, religion, national origin, age (over 40, in the US),
    disability, pregnancy and more, per statutes such as Title VII of the 1964 Civil Rights
    Act, the ADEA and the ADA — are off the table. Disability law adds the
    <strong>reasonable accommodation</strong> duty: adjusting the job, schedule or equipment
    so a qualified person can work, absent undue hardship. Wage-and-hour law (the 1938 Fair
    Labor Standards Act) sets minimum wage, overtime (time-and-a-half past 40 hours for
    non-exempt workers) and the classification line between employees and contractors —
    misclassification being a favourite enforcement target. Add leave law (the FMLA's
    12 weeks of job-protected unpaid leave for qualifying medical and family reasons),
    workplace safety (OSHA's general duty to provide a hazard-free workplace), privacy and
    monitoring limits, and the standalone prohibition on <strong>retaliation</strong> —
    punishing someone for complaining is unlawful even when the original complaint is wrong —
    and you have the architecture the widget sketches.
  </p>
  <p>
    The doctrine every HR course highlights is <strong>disparate impact</strong>, born from
    <em>Griggs v. Duke Power</em> (1971). Duke Power had required high-school diplomas and
    aptitude tests — neutral-sounding criteria introduced, the Court found, precisely to
    exclude Black workers after outright segregation became unlawful. Chief Justice Burger's
    rule: a facially neutral requirement is unlawful if it disproportionately excludes a
    protected group <em>and</em> is not demonstrably job-related ("business necessity").
    Discrimination without intent is discrimination still — and the burden shifts to the
    employer to prove the criterion actually predicts job performance. The operational
    instrument is the EEOC's <strong>four-fifths rule</strong>: compare selection rates across
    groups, and a rate below 80 per cent of the highest group's flags adverse impact worth
    validating. Worked example: 100 men and 100 women take a lifting test; 60 men and 36
    women pass — rates of 60 versus 36 per cent, an impact ratio of 0.6, well under 0.8 — so
    the test had better be a genuine, validated requirement of the job, or it is a lawsuit
    with arithmetic attached. This is why modern HR validates selection criteria with data,
    audits algorithms (which inherit the doctrine: a "neutral" model trained on biased
    history produces disparate impact at machine speed), and documents job-relatedness
    <em>before</em> deploying a filter. The principle has spread globally as outcome
    statistics became standard practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two further doctrines complete the floor, and both reward a worked passage.
    <strong>Harassment law</strong> recognises two forms under anti-discrimination statutes:
    <em>quid pro quo</em> (job benefits conditioned on sexual or romantic compliance — the
    supervisor's "promotion for dinner" case) and the <strong>hostile work environment</strong>
    — conduct severe or pervasive enough that a reasonable person would find the workplace
    intimidating, hostile or offensive, judged on the totality: frequency, severity, whether
    it was physically threatening, whether it interfered with work. The employer's legal
    position turns on process: an organisation that maintains a real policy, publicises a
    complaint channel, investigates promptly and acts on findings can escape liability for
    a supervisor's harassment (where no tangible job action occurred); an organisation whose
    policy was a poster and whose response was silence cannot. Harassment law is thus the
    clearest case of "process is protection": the paperwork <em>is</em> the defence, and the
    culture that makes people willing to complain is the prevention. <strong>Leave and
    accommodation law</strong> works the same logic in time: the FMLA's 12 weeks of
    job-protected unpaid leave (for employees of covered employers with a year of service
    and 1,250 hours worked) runs alongside the ADA's open-ended reasonable-accommodation
    duty, and the HR arithmetic is genuinely case-by-case: a worker needing six weeks off
    after surgery may be FMLA-eligible; a worker needing a permanently modified schedule may
    be an accommodation case; a worker needing "indefinite leave" is where both statutes
    famously run out — the interactive process, documented meeting by meeting, is what the
    law actually examines.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that employment law only bans deliberate prejudice. The law's reach is mostly <em>structural</em>: neutral practices — diploma filters, tenure-based pay, blanket criminal-record exclusions, the ripples of a biased algorithm — create liability when their measured impact falls on protected groups without job justification. Compliance is therefore a statistics-and-validation discipline, not a purity-of-heart exercise, which is exactly why HR exists as a profession rather than a policy memo.</p>

  <h2>Ethics and the HR profession: the dual loyalty</h2>
  <p>
    HR's central tension is built into the job description: HR is paid by the employer while
    safeguarding employees' rights — it holds salaries, health information, complaints and
    investigations in confidence, and it decides (or advises on) outcomes that change lives.
    Professional codes (SHRM's is the US standard: professional responsibility, ethical
    leadership, fairness and justice, conflicts of interest, use of information) exist
    because the function's value depends on <em>trust on both sides</em> — employees must
    believe HR will handle a harassment complaint lawfully and confidentially; leadership
    must believe HR will keep confidences and enforce standards even when the subject is a
    star performer. The recurring dilemmas are real: what HR owes a whistleblower versus the
    employer; when confidentiality must yield to a duty to act (credible safety threats,
    harassment the organisation is legally required to address); and the quiet daily version —
    documenting a manager's poor decision accurately in the file that will be read by a
    lawyer, not by the manager. The professional resolution is consistent: the law sets the
    floor, the code sets the professional standard, and <strong>process is protection</strong>
    for everyone — documentation, consistent application, progressive discipline and genuine
    voice are simultaneously the ethical standard, the legal defence and, the evidence says,
    the trust that makes people systems work at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four currents carry the foundation forward. <strong>The psychological contract</strong>:
    alongside the legal contract sits the unwritten one — expectations of fairness, growth
    and security — whose breach (organisational psychologists showed) predicts turnover and
    disengagement better than the written terms; much of modern HR is psychological-contract
    management. <strong>Global variation</strong>: employment systems differ deeply — German
    co-determination gives workers board seats and works councils real power; Japanese
    lifetime-employment norms reshape everything from hiring to dismissal; gig-economy law
    is re-litigating the employee/contractor line everywhere — so multinational HR is
    comparative law plus ethics, not policy copy-paste. <strong>The algorithmic frontier</strong>:
    AI screening, monitoring software and people analytics multiply both the power and the
    legal exposure — disparate impact at scale, privacy statutes (GDPR's automated-decision
    rights), and the professional question of who audits the auditor. <strong>Compliance as
    floor, not ceiling</strong>: the field's own evidence keeps showing that what predicts
    organisational performance — valid selection, credible voice, fair process, trust — is
    the <em>same</em> set of practices the law and ethics demand, which turns the old
    cynicism ("we do it because we must") inside out. The strategic case for HR is, at
    bottom, this convergence: the fair way of managing people and the effective way keep
    turning out to be the same way, and the profession's job is to prove it, decision by
    decision, with documentation.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> SHRM BASK; Griggs v. Duke Power Co. (1971); Dessler, <em>Human Resource Management</em>; Ulrich, <em>Human Resource Champions</em>.</p>
